.TH LEXVMF 1 "October 2026" "nylex 0.2"
.SH NAME
lexvmf \- list and change LEX system files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lexvmf \-l
.I file.vmf
.br
.B lexvmf \-x\fR|\fB\-t
.I file.vmf key
.br
.B lexvmf \-p\fR|\fB\-T
.I file.vmf key
[
.I file
]
.br
.B lexvmf \-d
.I file.vmf key
.br
.B lexvmf \-r
.I file.vmf
.SH DESCRIPTION
A system file (.vmf) of
.IR nylex (1)
or LEX holds records under keys of up to 10 characters: menus
(\c
.BR **MENU ,
.BR *DOCMENU ),
programs and forms, the installation (\c
.BR **INST ),
printers (\c
.BR **PNST ,
.BR *$ ...),
messages (\c
.BR **TEXT ,
.BR *-ERROR- xx),
document types (\c
.BR *.BRV ),
data records and, under
.BI : NAME EXT\fR,
the document index.
A record is stored squashed: a byte 1-31 is a run of that many squash
characters (\fB_\fR), a 0 byte ends a line.
.I Lexvmf
works on them.
.TP
.B \-l
lists the keys and the length of each record
.TP
.B \-x
writes the record to standard output, as it is stored
.TP
.B \-t
writes it as text: the runs opened, a newline for each 0 byte
.TP
.B \-p
puts a record in, from
.I file
or standard input, replacing one with the same key; it makes the system
file if there is none
.TP
.B \-T
puts a record in from text (newlines become 0 bytes, runs of
.B _
are squashed)
.TP
.B \-d
deletes a record (it is only marked deleted, as LEX does)
.TP
.B \-r
rewrites the file without the deleted records, which packs it
(nylex's menu option /3 does the same with questions)
.PP
Keys are padded with blanks to 10 characters; \e\fBx\fINN\fR in a key is
the byte
.IR NN ,
in hexadecimal.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
lexvmf -l $HOME/nylex.vmf
lexvmf -t $HOME/nylex.vmf '**MENU'
lexvmf -t $HOME/nylex.vmf '*VDTI' > vdti.txt; vi vdti.txt
lexvmf -T $HOME/nylex.vmf '*VDTI' vdti.txt
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
nylex(1).
The source distribution's lexdef.txt and tools/mklexvmf.py make a whole
system file from a text description.
